    "text": "A refrigerator works by moving heat from inside to outside, like carrying warmth away in a bucket. A special liquid called refrigerant flows through pipes. First, it evaporates inside the fridge, soaking up heat from your food and air. That turns it into a gas. Then a compressor squeezes the gas, making it hot. Next, the hot gas flows through coils on the back or bottom, where it releases that heat into your kitchen and turns back into a liquid. Finally, it cycles back inside to absorb more heat. Because heat keeps getting removed, the inside stays cold, keeping your food fresh."
